#### 1.1.1 The equalities (1.6) and (1.7) are obvious. The ellipticity condition (1.14) stems from (2.16) and we have already checked (1.3) and (1.4). In view of (2.17) and Lemma 2.1, this means that all the assumptions of Theorem 1.1 are satisfied. 

_Remark 2.1_.: If \(\Gamma\) is of class \(\mathcal{C}^{2}\) (resp. if \(\Omega\) is a convex polygon) Theorem I.5.4 (resp. Remark I.5.6) says that \(\mathbf{u}\!\in\!W^{2,\cdot\,r}(\Omega)^{2}\) and \(p\!\in\!W^{1,\cdot\,r}(\Omega)\) as long as \(\mathbf{f}\) belongs to \(L^{r}(\Omega)^{2}\). Therefore, this mild regularity assumption on \(\mathbf{f}\) and \(\Omega\) is sufficient to ensure that Problem (\(\tilde{\mathbf{Q}}\)) has a unique solution.

